Stage 4 – Defend the Design (Joint) – 2 Marks
1. Recommended thread pool size
A fixed thread pool controls the number of active worker threads.
- 1 thread: Processes files one by one. It is reliable but may take more time.
- 2 threads: Allows two files to be processed simultaneously.
- 4 threads: Provides more parallelism and may improve performance.
- 8 threads: Can improve performance further, but may also create additional overhead.
For this file-processing application, I would initially recommend 4 threads, then compare the actual measurements and choose the fastest configuration that remains stable.

More threads do not always improve performance because:
- All threads compete for disk resources.
- Creating and scheduling threads adds overhead.
- Too many threads can increase memory usage.
- The disk may become a bottleneck even when more threads are available.
2. How does the design guarantee a complete and correct summary?
1. The program creates a task for every expected file.
2. Each task independently reads and validates its file.
3. A file-specific exception is converted into a failure result.
4. The Future.get() method waits for each submitted task to finish.
5. Successful and failed results are collected.
6. The program counts both categories after task completion.
7. It checks whether the number of collected results equals 10,000.
The summary must satisfy:
\[
10000 = \text{Successful Files} + \text{Failed Files}
\]

Important: This guarantee applies when processing completes without interruption or an unrecoverable JVM failure. If processing is interrupted, the program must report an incomplete summary rather than claim that all files were processed.
3. Remaining limitation
The program processes each file independently, but it does not save the calculated results permanently in a database. If the application crashes, the processing may need to be repeated.

A future improvement would be to save each successful result and failure record in a database or log file. This would support recovery and avoid losing completed work.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
The same 10,000 files could be processed using parallel streams or by creating one thread per file.
1. ExecutorService
- Uses a fixed number of worker threads.
- Reuses threads instead of creating a new thread for every file.
- Provides control over pool size.
- Uses Callable and Future to retrieve task results.
- Individual file exceptions can be caught and recorded.
If \(N\) is the number of files and \(P\) is the number of worker threads, ideal processing time is approximately:
\[
T = O\left(\frac{N \times W}{P}\right)
\]

Here, \(W\) represents the average processing work for one file. Actual time also includes I/O, scheduling and synchronization overhead.

Memory for worker thread stacks is approximately:
\[
M = O(P \times S)
\]

where \(S\) is the stack size of one thread. There is also memory for task objects, futures and results.
2. Parallel Streams
Example:
files.parallelStream().forEach(file -> {
    try {
        processFile(file);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        System.out.println(
            "Failed: " + file.getName());
    }
});


- Uses parallel processing through Java's common ForkJoinPool by default.
- Does not require manually creating one thread for each file.
- The number of worker threads is generally based on available processors and pool configuration.
- An exception that is not caught inside the file-processing operation can cause the stream operation to fail.
- Catching exceptions inside the operation allows other files to continue processing.
Its ideal processing time is approximately \(O(NW/P)\), but actual performance depends on the common pool, file I/O and available resources.

Memory for worker thread stacks is approximately \(O(P \times S)\), in addition to task and result storage.
3. Creating one thread per file
Example:
for (File file : files) {
    Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
        try {
            processFile(file);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(
                "Failed: " + file.getName());
        }
    });

    t.start();
}


- Creates 10,000 threads for 10,000 files.
- Every thread has its own stack memory.
- Thread creation and scheduling create high overhead.
- It can cause memory exhaustion or native-thread resource errors.
- A failure must be caught inside each thread; otherwise, that thread terminates without producing a useful failure record.
- Collecting results safely also requires thread-safe data structures and proper synchronization.
The approximate thread-stack memory requirement is:
\[
M = O(10000 \times S)
\]

For example, if the configured stack size is 1 MB per thread:
\[
10000 \times 1\text{ MB} = 10000\text{ MB}
\]

That is approximately 10 GB of stack address-space reservation. Actual committed physical memory may be lower, but creating that many threads is still risky.
4. Comparison table
Feature	ExecutorService	Parallel Stream	One thread per file
Thread creation	Reuses pool threads	Uses common pool	Creates 10,000 threads
Thread control	High	Limited by default	Very high overhead
Failure handling	Individual task handling	Catch inside operation	Catch inside each thread
Memory usage	Controlled by pool size	Controlled by pool	Very high
Result collection	Future and concurrent collection	Requires safe collection	Requires safe collection
Suitability	Highly suitable	Suitable for simpler tasks	Not recommended

5. Final complexity comparison
Let:
- \(N\) = number of files (10,000)
- \(P\) = number of worker threads
- \(S\) = stack size per thread
- \(W\) = average work per file
Approach	Ideal processing time	Thread stack memory
ExecutorService	\(O(NW/P)\)	\(O(PS)\)
Parallel Stream	\(O(NW/P)\)	\(O(PS)\)
One thread per file	\(O(W)\) idealized	\(O(NS)\)

The one-thread-per-file time is only an idealized lower bound assuming all tasks run simultaneously. In practice, operating-system scheduling, disk contention, thread creation and resource limits can make it much slower.
Conclusion
The proposed Result Processing System uses ExecutorService, Callable, Future and ConcurrentLinkedQueue to process 10,000 student score files concurrently. It handles missing and corrupt files independently, so one bad file does not stop the entire process. It also produces a summary of successful and failed files.

ExecutorService is the recommended approach because it controls the number of threads, reuses worker threads, reduces memory overhead and provides reliable task-result collection.

